To implement Option 1 using the Agile Deployment Toolkit (ADT) datastore scripts, pair a local-disk Debezium Server (or CDC exporter) with PutToDatastore.sh in "distributed" mode on the primary region, and poll your local region's bucket with GetFromDatastore.sh on the remote region.
Because ADT's datastore wrapper scripts natively replicate "distributed" writes across every pipe-delimited (|) S3 endpoint configured in your template, you do not need cross-region S3 replication or custom multi-bucket upload logic.

1. Configure Multi-Region Datastore Chains in ADT

In your ADT templates, define S3_ACCESS_KEY, S3_SECRET_KEY, S3_HOST_BASE, and S3_LOCATION using pipe-delimited chains. ADT always treats the first entry (.s5cfg-1) as the local region's reference bucket.
• Primary Region Template (e.g., London primary, Amsterdam secondary):bash
export S3_ACCESS_KEY="<LON_KEY>|<AMS_KEY>"
export S3_SECRET_KEY="<LON_SECRET>|<AMS_SECRET>"
export S3_HOST_BASE="://linodeobjects.com|://linodeobjects.com"
export S3_LOCATION="US|US"
Use code with caution.
• Remote Region Template (Amsterdam first so local reads hit Amsterdam):bash
export S3_ACCESS_KEY="<AMS_KEY>|<LON_KEY>"
export S3_SECRET_KEY="<AMS_SECRET>|<LON_SECRET>"
export S3_HOST_BASE="://linodeobjects.com|://linodeobjects.com"
export S3_LOCATION="US|US"
Use code with caution.
(Refer to the ADT Datastore Operational Protocols for exact multi-region chain behavior).

2. Primary Region: Capture CDC & Push with PutToDatastore.sh

Instead of having Debezium write directly to S3, configure Debezium Server to write JSON change events to a local staging directory (e.g., /var/tmp/cdc-out/), rotating files every few seconds with sequence-prefixed filenames (00000001.jsonl, 00000002.jsonl).
Then run a lightweight shipper loop (or cron job) on your primary-region database/worker node that mounts your CDC bucket across regions and pushes each completed batch using PutToDatastore.sh in distributed mode: [1] (https://www.wintersys-initiatives.uk/Agile%20Deployment%20Toolkit/Operations/AuthenticatorMachines)
bash
#!/bin/sh
BUCKET_NAME="cdc-tx-stream"
STAGE_DIR="/var/tmp/cdc-out"

# Ensure the bucket exists across all configured regions
${HOME}/services/datastore/operations/MountDatastore.sh "${BUCKET_NAME}" "distributed"

for file in $(ls -1 "${STAGE_DIR}"/*.jsonl 2>/dev/null | sort); do
    filename=$(basename "${file}")
    
    # Push to ALL regions in the S3 chain ("distributed") and delete local file ("yes")
    ${HOME}/services/datastore/operations/PutToDatastore.sh \
        "${BUCKET_NAME}" \
        "${file}" \
        "tx/${filename}" \
        "distributed" \
        "yes"
done
Use code with caution.
• Why "distributed" matters: ADT loops through every configured S3 endpoint and uploads the transaction batch to both your primary and remote Linode Object Storage buckets in one call.

3. Remote Region: Pull & Apply with GetFromDatastore.sh

On the remote region's database/worker node, poll your local region's replicated bucket (://linodeobjects.com) using ListFromDatastore.sh and GetFromDatastore.sh. Because GetFromDatastore.sh always reads from the first credential in your chain (.s5cfg-1), cross-region network fetches are avoided during replay.
bash
#!/bin/sh
BUCKET_NAME="cdc-tx-stream"
WORK_DIR="/var/tmp/cdc-in"
STATE_FILE="${HOME}/runtime/last_applied_tx.seq"
mkdir -p "${WORK_DIR}"
touch "${STATE_FILE}"

LAST_APPLIED=$(cat "${STATE_FILE}")

# List objects in the local region bucket and sort lexicographically
OBJECTS=$(${HOME}/services/datastore/operations/ListFromDatastore.sh "${BUCKET_NAME}" "tx/" | awk '{print $NF}' | sort)

for obj_path in ${OBJECTS}; do
    filename=$(basename "${obj_path}")
    [ -z "${filename}" ] && continue

    # Skip already-applied batches
    if [ "${filename}" \> "${LAST_APPLIED}" ]; then
        local_file="${WORK_DIR}/${filename}"

        # Pull from local region datastore
        ${HOME}/services/datastore/operations/GetFromDatastore.sh \
            "${BUCKET_NAME}" \
            "tx/${filename}" \
            "${local_file}"

        if [ -f "${local_file}" ]; then
            # Convert Debezium JSON events into idempotent SQL and execute
            python3 ${HOME}/utilities/cdc/apply_debezium_batch.py "${local_file}" && {
                echo "${filename}" > "${STATE_FILE}"
                rm -f "${local_file}"
            }
        fi
    fi
done
Use code with caution.

4. Connecting the Applier to Linode Managed Databases in ADT

In ADT, DBaaS credentials and connection helpers are already wired into your deployed nodes: [1] (https://www.wintersys-initiatives.uk/Agile%20Deployment%20Toolkit/Deployment/ConnectingToCurrentDatabase)
• Database Connection: Inside apply_debezium_batch.py (or a shell wrapper), source ADT's database configuration variables or invoke ADT's existing connection wrappers (${HOME}/utilities/remote/ConnectToRemotePostgresDB.sh or ConnectToMySQLDB.sh) to get the remote Managed Database host, port, username, password, and SSL certificate path. [1] (https://www.wintersys-initiatives.uk/Agile%20Deployment%20Toolkit/Deployment/ConnectingToCurrentDatabase)
• Firewall Access: Ensure the remote worker node's IP (or VPC range) is allowed on your remote Linode Managed Database firewall—similar to how ADT's Multi-Region Operations manages cross-region DBaaS allow lists.
• Cleanup: Schedule a periodic retention cleanup on the remote node using DeleteFromDatastore.sh (in "local" or "distributed" mode) once objects are older than your recovery window.
If you'd like the ready-to-use application.properties for Debezium Server and the apply_debezium_batch.py applier script, let me know:
• Whether your Managed Database is MySQL or PostgreSQL
• Which ADT machine role (webserver, database node, or dedicated worker) you plan to run the shipper and applier on
